---
import AtlasGlyph from "../ui/AtlasGlyph.astro";
import SourcePanel from "./SourcePanel.astro";

const { topic } = Astro.props;

const facts = [
  ["Also known as", "Age of Heroes"],
  ["Duration", "864,000 human years"],
  ["Position", "Third age of every Maha Yuga"],
  ["Dharma", "Two legs remain"],
];

const dharmaLegs = ["Compassion", "Discipline"];

const symbols = [
  ["Sudarshana Chakra", "Krishna's Divine Discus", "A symbol of decisive justice, used to protect Dharma rather than to seek conquest for its own sake."],
  ["Kurukshetra", "The Field of Dharma", "Where the Bhagavad Gita is spoken, and where duty, grief, and right action are tested in the middle of war."],
  ["Bhagavad Gita", "Krishna's Teaching to Arjuna", "Guidance delivered in the middle of conflict, showing that clarity is often found within difficulty, not apart from it."],
  ["Dwarka", "Krishna's Ocean Kingdom", "A prosperous, protected city that tradition describes as reclaimed by the sea after Krishna's departure, closing the age."],
];

const lifeChapters = [
  ["Birth in Mathura", "Born to Devaki and Vasudeva, then carried in secret to Gokul to escape the tyranny of Kansa."],
  ["Childhood in Vrindavan", "Remembered through play, protection of the vulnerable, and the devotion shared between Krishna and those around him."],
  ["Return and restoration", "Krishna returns to Mathura, ends Kansa's rule, and later establishes the kingdom of Dwarka."],
  ["Kurukshetra and the Gita", "As Arjuna's charioteer, Krishna delivers the Bhagavad Gita and guides the outcome of the Mahabharata war."],
];
---

<article class="dvapara-reading">
  <header class="dvapara-hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">Four Yugas / Third Age</p>
      <h1>Dvapara Yuga</h1>
      <p class="meaning">The Age of Heroes</p>
      <p class="lead">Duty is no longer effortless, and it is no longer simply followed by command. In Dvapara Yuga, virtue and conflict grow equally visible, and the world begins to need guidance to tell them apart.</p>
      <div class="hero-links">
        <a href="#dvapara-article">Enter the age</a>
        <a href="/atlas/four-yugas">Four Yugas map</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="hero-card" aria-label="Dvapara Yuga summary">
      <div class="hero-glyph"><AtlasGlyph name="four-yugas" size={54} /></div>
      <p>This is the age of the Mahabharata and Krishna: powerful dynasties, deep loyalties, difficult choices, and a war fought over what Dharma actually requires.</p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <div data-reading-body class="article-shell" id="dvapara-article">
    <article class="prose-flow">
      <section class="opening-scene">
        <p class="eyebrow">Opening vision</p>
        <h2>Imagine a world where good and difficult are no longer easy to separate</h2>
        <p>Dvapara Yuga is remembered as the age where the clean simplicity of earlier ages gives way to real complexity. Kingdoms grow larger, alliances grow more tangled, and even admirable people carry real flaws.</p>
        <p>Dharma has not disappeared. But it can no longer be read off the surface of events. It has to be discerned, argued over, and sometimes chosen at great personal cost.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">The world of Dvapara Yuga</p>
        <h2>Powerful dynasties, deep bonds, and rising conflict</h2>
        <p>Traditional descriptions place Dvapara Yuga as an age of great kingdoms, skilled warriors, and complicated family ties. Life is materially rich and socially advanced, but attachment, pride, and rivalry grow stronger alongside virtue.</p>
        <p>This is not portrayed as a fall into darkness. It is portrayed as an age where heroism becomes necessary, because things worth protecting are no longer protected automatically.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
        <h2>The bridge between duty and struggle</h2>
        <p>Dvapara Yuga sits at the midpoint of the Four Yugas. It carries forward the sense of responsibility built in Treta Yuga, while beginning to show the strain that defines Kali Yuga.</p>
        <p>For this reason, it is often remembered less for simple ideals and more for hard questions: what does loyalty ask of us, what does justice cost, and what should a person do when every choice available is imperfect.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Name and meaning</p>
        <h2>Why it is called Dvapara Yuga</h2>
        <p><strong>Dvapara</strong> is connected to the idea of two, or of doubt. Many traditions read this as pointing to a world in which truth and untruth exist side by side, and discernment is needed to tell them apart.</p>
        <div class="note-card">
          <strong>Two legs remain</strong>
          <p>Where Treta Yuga stood on three legs of Dharma, Dvapara Yuga is described as standing on two. Righteousness is still present and still powerful, but it must now share the world with real doubt and real conflict.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="fact-strip" aria-label="Dvapara Yuga quick facts">
        {facts.map(([label, value]) => <div><span>{label}</span><strong>{value}</strong></div>)}
      </section>

      <section class="dharma-section">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Dharma on two legs</p>
          <h2>The Bull of Dharma stands on half its strength</h2>
          <p>In the common symbolism of the Yugas, the Bull of Dharma loses one leg with each age. By Dvapara Yuga, only two of the four original legs remain standing.</p>
          <p>This is why the age feels different from Satya or Treta Yuga. Dharma is not absent, but it is contested. It must be actively upheld rather than simply inherited.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="dharma-card">
          <div class="meter"><span></span><span></span><span class="dim"></span><span class="dim"></span></div>
          <strong>2 / 4</strong>
          <small>Dharma contested</small>
          <ul>{dharmaLegs.map((leg) => <li>{leg}</li>)}</ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="symbols-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">Symbols of Dvapara Yuga</p>
        <h2>The age becomes easier to remember through its living symbols</h2>
        <p>Dvapara Yuga is carried in memory through the imagery of the Mahabharata: a battlefield that becomes a place of teaching, a discus that protects rather than conquers, and a kingdom that does not outlast the one who built it.</p>
        <div class="symbol-grid">
          {symbols.map(([name, title, text]) => (
            <article>
              <span>{name.slice(0, 1)}</span>
              <strong>{name}</strong>
              <small>{title}</small>
              <p>{text}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Human life</p>
        <h2>Strength and struggle grow together</h2>
        <p>Ancient sources describe Dvapara Yuga as an age of great capability. People are strong, intelligent, and accomplished, yet also more driven by desire, pride, and attachment than in earlier ages.</p>
        <p>Memory and lifespan are described as reduced compared to Treta Yuga. Rules, treaties, and codes of conduct become more necessary, because shared values can no longer be assumed without effort.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Living knowledge</p>
        <h2>Wisdom begins to be organized and written down</h2>
        <p>Dvapara Yuga marks a turning point in how sacred knowledge is preserved. Tradition credits the sage Krishna Dvaipayana, known as Vyasa, with arranging the single Veda into four during this age, and with compiling the Mahabharata and the Puranas.</p>
        <div class="flow-line"><span>Living memory</span><i></i><span>Vyasa</span><i></i><span>Organized texts</span></div>
        <p>Where Satya Yuga relied on direct transmission alone, Dvapara Yuga begins to rely on structured compilation, so that knowledge could survive a world where memory itself was weakening.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">A great sage</p>
        <h2>Vyasa, the compiler of ages</h2>
        <p>Vyasa stands at the center of Dvapara Yuga's intellectual life. Beyond arranging the Vedas, he is traditionally regarded as the author of the Mahabharata, of which the Bhagavad Gita forms a central part.</p>
        <p>Through Vyasa, this age is remembered not only for war and heroism, but for the deliberate preservation of Dharma in written and organized form.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Divine beings</p>
        <h2>Krishna and the idea of a complete avatar</h2>
        <p>Many Vaishnava traditions describe Krishna as a Purna Avatara, a complete manifestation of Vishnu, distinct from the more partial incarnations associated with earlier ages.</p>
        <p>Krishna's presence in Dvapara Yuga is not only that of a warrior or statesman. He appears as friend, guide, strategist, and teacher, shaping the age through relationship as much as through action.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">Avatar and story</p>
        <h2>The life of Krishna, in four chapters</h2>
        <div class="avatar-list">
          {lifeChapters.map(([name, text]) => <article><strong>{name}</strong><p>{text}</p></article>)}
        </div>
        <p>These chapters connect a personal life story to the largest events of the age: a childhood of devotion, a return that restores order, and a war in which the deepest teaching arrives in the middle of grief.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">A nuanced age</p>
        <h2>Heroes with real flaws, not flawless heroes</h2>
        <p>The Mahabharata is remembered as much for its moral complexity as for its heroism. Yudhishthira, admired for truthfulness, still gambles away a kingdom. Bhishma, bound by a vow, stays silent while Dharma is tested. Karna's loyalty and Duryodhana's ambition both come from recognisable human motives, not simple villainy.</p>
        <p>This is one of the clearest markers of Dvapara Yuga: virtue and failure are shown living inside the same people, not divided neatly between opposing sides.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">The transition</p>
        <h2>How Dvapara Yuga gives way to Kali Yuga</h2>
        <p>The Kurukshetra war brings the age to its turning point. Traditional accounts describe immense loss, including the near-total end of the Kshatriya lineages central to the epic.</p>
        <p>Puranic tradition further describes Krishna's eventual departure from the world as marking the formal beginning of Kali Yuga. Where Treta Yuga ended gradually, Dvapara Yuga is remembered as ending through a decisive and costly turning point.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-scene">
        <p class="eyebrow">What it teaches</p>
        <h2>Dvapara Yuga is the memory of guided struggle</h2>
        <p>Dvapara Yuga represents an age where strength, loyalty, and love coexist with pride, rivalry, and loss.</p>
        <p>It reminds us that clarity rarely arrives on its own once complexity sets in. It asks what guidance we turn to, and what side of ourselves we choose to strengthen, when right and wrong stop being obvious.</p>
      </section>

      <details class="sources-drawer">
        <summary><span>Sources and editorial notes</span><b>Open references</b></summary>
        <SourcePanel sources={topic.data.sources} sourceNote={topic.data.sourceNote} traditionNote={topic.data.traditionNote} reviewedBy={topic.data.reviewedBy} lastReviewed={topic.data.lastReviewed} contentVersion={topic.data.contentVersion} />
      </details>

      <a class="continue" href="/atlas/kali-yuga">
        <span><small>Continue the journey</small><strong>Next: Kali Yuga</strong><em>The fourth and present age in the common Puranic model.</em></span>
        <b>Open Kali Yuga -></b>
      </a>
    </article>
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  .dvapara-reading {
    --accent: light-dark(rgb(60 62 110 / 1), #8c91ff);
    --gold: light-dark(rgb(100 78 41 / 1), #e9b65f);
    --warm: light-dark(rgb(43 33 24 / 1), #f4e6cf);
    color: var(--warm);
    border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.16), rgb(140 145 255 / .16));
    border-radius: 1rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: linear-gradient(145deg, light-dark(rgb(250 245 234 / 0.7), rgb(14 12 30 / .7)), light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.54), rgb(3 8 20 / .54)));
  }

  .dvapara-hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 19rem;
    gap: 1rem;
    width: min(100% - 2rem, 68rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 3.2rem) 0 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.12), rgb(140 145 255 / .12));
  }

  .hero-text { max-width: 46rem; }
  .eyebrow { margin: 0; color: var(--gold); font-size: .62rem; font-weight: 850; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  h1, h2, h3 { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 450; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  h1 { margin: .35rem 0 0; font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 5vw, 4.9rem); line-height: .98; }
  .meaning { margin: .45rem 0 0; color: var(--accent); font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 1.7vw, 1.45rem); }
  .lead { max-width: 52ch; margin: .9rem 0 0; color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #d0c8bd); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.68; }

  .hero-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .55rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .hero-links a, .continue { text-decoration: none; }
  .hero-links a { border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.24), rgb(233 182 95 / .24)); border-radius: 999px; padding: .58rem .85rem; color: light-dark(rgb(104 92 68 / 1), #f3d79e); font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 850; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .hero-links a:first-child { background: rgb(233 182 95 / .1); }

  .hero-card { align-self: end; border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.18), rgb(140 145 255 / .18)); border-radius: 1rem; padding: 1rem; background: radial-gradient(circle at top right, rgb(140 145 255 / .12), transparent 48%), light-dark(rgb(99 76 44 / 0.018), rgb(255 255 255 / .018)); }
  .hero-glyph { display: grid; width: 4.6rem; height: 4.6rem; place-items: center; border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.38), rgb(140 145 255 / .38)); border-radius: 50%; color: var(--accent); }
  .hero-card p { margin: .9rem 0 0; color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #aaa49a); line-height: 1.55; }

  .article-shell { width: min(100% - 2rem, 68rem); margin: 0 auto; }
  .prose-flow { max-width: 48rem; padding: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .prose-flow section { padding-block: clamp(1.55rem, 3vw, 2.7rem); border-bottom: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.1), rgb(140 145 255 / .1)); }
  .prose-flow h2 { max-width: 25ch; margin: .35rem 0 .75rem; font-size: clamp(1.65rem, 2.5vw, 2.35rem); line-height: 1.12; }
  .prose-flow p { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #aaa49a); line-height: 1.72; }
  .prose-flow strong { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #eee3cf); }

  .opening-scene, .closing-scene { border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.16), rgb(140 145 255 / .16)); border-radius: 1rem; padding: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 2rem) !important; background: radial-gradient(circle at top left, rgb(140 145 255 / .1), transparent 44%), light-dark(rgb(99 76 44 / 0.014), rgb(255 255 255 / .014)); }

  .fact-strip { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: .5rem; }
  .fact-strip div { border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.14), rgb(140 145 255 / .14)); border-radius: .8rem; padding: .7rem .8rem; background: light-dark(rgb(99 76 44 / 0.018), rgb(255 255 255 / .018)); }
  .fact-strip span { display: block; color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #99938a); font-size: .55rem; font-weight: 850; letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .fact-strip strong { display: block; margin-top: .18rem; color: var(--warm); font-family: var(--serif); font-size: .98rem; font-weight: 500; }

  .note-card, .dharma-card, .avatar-list article, .symbol-grid article { border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.18), rgb(140 145 255 / .18)); border-radius: .9rem; padding: .9rem; background: light-dark(rgb(99 76 44 / 0.018), rgb(255 255 255 / .018)); }
  .note-card { margin-top: .9rem; }
  .note-card p { margin: .35rem 0 0; }

  .dharma-section { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 13rem; gap: 1rem; align-items: start; }
  .dharma-card { text-align: center; }
  .meter { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: .32rem; }
  .meter span { height: .5rem; border-radius: 999px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--accent), #f3d79e); }
  .meter span.dim { background: light-dark(rgb(99 76 44 / 0.09), rgb(255 255 255 / .09)); }
  .dharma-card strong { display: block; margin-top: .55rem; font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 1.45rem; font-weight: 400; }
  .dharma-card small { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #99938a); font-size: .66rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .dharma-card ul { display: grid; gap: .35rem; margin: .8rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .dharma-card li { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #d8c8ab); font-size: .82rem; }

  .flow-line { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; margin: .9rem 0; }
  .flow-line span { border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.22), rgb(140 145 255 / .22)); border-radius: 999px; padding: .42rem .65rem; color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #ead7b8); font-size: .82rem; }
  .flow-line i { width: 1.2rem; height: 1px; background: rgb(140 145 255 / .45); }

  .avatar-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .55rem; margin: .9rem 0; }
  .avatar-list strong { display: block; font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .avatar-list p { margin: .2rem 0 0; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.55; }

  .sources-drawer { border: 0; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .sources-drawer > summary { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: .75rem; border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.18), rgb(140 145 255 / .18)); border-radius: .85rem; padding: .8rem .95rem; background: rgb(140 145 255 / .045); color: var(--accent); cursor: pointer; list-style: none; }
  .sources-drawer > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .sources-drawer span { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #eee3cf); font-weight: 800; }
  .sources-drawer b { color: light-dark(rgb(104 92 68 / 1), #f3d79e); font-size: .66rem; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .sources-drawer :global(.source-panel) { margin-top: .75rem; }

  .continue { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; margin-block: 1rem 1.5rem; border: 1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.22), rgb(233 182 95 / .22)); border-radius: .95rem; padding: 1rem; color: light-dark(rgb(104 92 68 / 1), #f3d79e); background: linear-gradient(100deg, rgb(233 182 95 / .055), transparent); }
  .continue span > * { display: block; }
  .continue small { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #99938a); font-size: .58rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .continue strong { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 400; }
  .continue em { color: light-dark(rgb(91 76 57 / 1), #aaa49a); font-size: .75rem; font-style: normal; }
  .continue b { align-self: center; font-size: .66rem; text-transform: uppercase; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) { .dvapara-hero, .dharma-section { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .hero-card { align-self: stretch; } .fact-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  @media (max-width: 620px) { .fact-strip, .avatar-list, .symbol-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .continue { display: grid; } }
</style>
